Browser evaluation of the page served from this repository on 4879, opened with the link the
command printed. Chrome on macOS, dark scheme, window 614 CSS px wide, through the Claude in
Chrome extension; values are from the live DOM and screenshots. This is the real machine, not a
staged one: five coding-shell sessions were open in four Soft Foundry repositories (four claude,
one grok), none started by this work. The other three repositories' names, branches, and changes
are not written here.

Observed at the working tree that became 952808b. Two faults were found in this pass and fixed
before that commit: the Running view failed to draw (a helper shadowed by a local variable; the
page said "Not connected"), and a link carrying the token did nothing when opened in a tab that
already had the page.

EVAL-006 the home page answers "what is going on, and where"
  Opening the printed link: title "Repositories · Soft Foundry"; the address bar then shows
    http://127.0.0.1:4879/#/ with no token in it.
  nav: Repositories (current), Running. No Changes or Workflow at the top any more.
  lede: "4 Soft Foundry repositories on this machine: the one this page was started in, and every
    one with a session or a command running. 5 coding-shell sessions are open across them."
  Four cards, each with a molten top edge (something is running in each):
    soft_foundry, ~/dev/soft_foundry, "This page was started here."; Sessions: "claude in ttys009
      on change/ui-repositories: ui-repositories, phase intake"; Commands running: "soft-foundry ui";
      Open changes (6), each a link with its recorded status and phase; "18 closed. All changes and
      gates in soft_foundry".
    three more, each naming its sessions with terminal and branch, its open changes (1, 1, and 0),
      and a link to all its changes. One has two claude sessions on a branch with no change record.

EVAL-007 into another repository and back
  Clicking another repository's name: #/r/<id>/changes; title "Changes · <repo> · Soft Foundry";
    a bar at the top reads "Repositories / <repo> ~/…/<repo>" with tabs Changes (current) and
    Workflow; h1 "Changes in <repo>"; "In this repository now: 1 coding-shell session. See
    everything that is running"; its one open change carries the flag "Session open: grok".
  Clicking that change: #/r/<id>/change/<slug>; the same bar; sixteen gates; the note says a grok
    session is open on it and in which terminal. (That record has no handoffs, so every gate reads
    Missing and the page says 16 gates are failing: that is the record's real state.)
  Workflow tab: #/r/<id>/workflow; h1 "Workflow in <repo>"; that repository's sixteen phases.
  "Repositories" in the bar returns to the home page.

EVAL-008 Running links to where each thing is
  lede: "5 coding-shell sessions are open across 4 Soft Foundry repositories on this machine. 1 Soft
    Foundry command is running besides this page."
  Sessions table: five rows; every row's Repository cell is a link to that repository; the three
    rows whose branch has a change record link to that change in its own repository; the two on a
    branch without one say so.
  Clicking a session's change in another repository lands on #/r/<id>/change/<slug> with the
    repository bar and the session note.

EVAL-009 the token
  With the tab's stored token removed and the page reloaded at http://127.0.0.1:4879/: h1 "Open the
    link from your terminal", an explanation, and no repository shown. A fetch of /api/repositories
    without the header answers 401.
  Restoring the token restores the page.

EVAL-010 accessibility of the new parts
  The repository bar is a nav landmark labelled "Repository"; the current tab has aria-current.
  Each card's repository name is an h2 link; Sessions, Commands running, Open changes are h3.
  No sideways page scroll at 614 px on the home page, a board, a change, the workflow, or Running.
  Colours are the existing palette. "This page was started here", "No coding shell is open here",
    and the session and change lines are all words.

Not exercised: a screen reader; the light scheme; 200% zoom; a repository found only because a
soft-foundry command (not a session) is running in it, in a browser (covered by a server test).
